; Cranelisp — finding what the library already has
; "Is there already a function for this?" — answered before you import.

; /imports shows what is in scope right now. /list shows what you have
; defined. But the standard library is much larger than what is imported.
; /search looks across every module reachable on the lib path that is NOT
; yet in scope — "importable, but not yet imported".

/imports

; --- Field accessors: every record field is a function -------------------
; A product type gives you a canonical accessor named Type.field. /list
; shows what you have defined — here the accessor by its canonical
; qualified name:

(deftype Box [:primitives/Int v])
/list

; Box.v is the canonical accessor; the bare field name v is a convenience
; alias for it. Both pull the field out of a Box:

(Box.v (Box 42))
(v (Box 42))

; --- Searching the library -----------------------------------------------
; Search by NAME. An exact name, or any substring of a name, matches.
; "bit" finds the whole bitwise family in num.bits — and each row tells you
; four things: the :Type signature, the name, the module it lives in, and
; the exact (import ...) form to bring it into scope.

/search bit

; A substring need not be a prefix. "count" finds bit-count and popcount —
; the match is anywhere in the name:

/search count

; Search by TYPE SIGNATURE. Give /search a type shape and it finds every
; importable symbol of that shape, up to renaming of type variables. Here:
; every reachable unary Int -> Int function, across two modules.

/search (Fn [primitives/Int] primitives/Int)

; Short type names work too — the query is matched structurally, not as
; text. (Fn [Int Int] Int) finds the same set as the fully-qualified form:

/search (Fn [Int Int] Int)

; A type need not be the whole signature — a partial shape matches any
; signature that CONTAINS it. "String" finds anything whose type mentions
; String anywhere:

/search String

; Found one you want? Copy the import form straight from the result row.
; /search told us popcount lives in num.bits:

(import [num.bits [popcount]])
(popcount 255)

; Now popcount is in scope — so it is no longer "importable but not in
; scope", and /search stops listing it. /search only ever shows what you
; could still pull in:

/search popcount

; A query that matches nothing is answered plainly — never an opaque error:

/search no-such-function-anywhere

; --- New project? The config template writes itself ----------------------
; Point cranelisp at a fresh project directory and it scaffolds a
; Cranelisp.toml for you — the cargo-new / git-init ergonomic. The notice
; appears at startup. (Shown here via a shell escape spawning a nested REPL;
; the nested banner is filtered so only the scaffold notice shows.)

/sh mkdir -p myproject && printf '/quit\n' | $CRANELISP_PLATFORM_PATH/cranelisp myproject 2>&1 | grep created

; The generated file is a teaching template: a commented header and a
; commented-out lib-dirs example. Because the lib-dir model is additive,
; an all-commented file changes resolution by exactly nothing — it is a
; pure template, safe by construction:

/sh sed -n '1,9p' myproject/Cranelisp.toml

; Launch on the same project again and it is a silent no-op — an existing
; config is never overwritten. No create notice the second time:

/sh printf '/quit\n' | $CRANELISP_PLATFORM_PATH/cranelisp myproject 2>&1 | grep created || echo '(no second create — existing config left untouched)'
